    {
      "id": "GENUS_VOMBATUS",
      "name": "Vombatus",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "Common wombats — barrel-shaped, powerfully built marsupials that are the world's largest burrowing herbivores. A wombat can dig a burrow tunnel over 30 m long and 2 m deep. Their backward-facing pouch (unique among marsupials) prevents soil from entering while digging. Wombats are the only animals known to produce cubic faeces — a result of non-uniform elasticity in the last section of the intestine, which shapes the droppings as they dry over a 2-3 week digestion time. The cubic shape prevents the droppings rolling away from marking posts, believed to be important in territorial communication. During the 2020 Australian bushfires, wombats reportedly allowed other animals to shelter in their burrows, leading to the romantic but unverified 'wombat hero' story.",
      "lineage": "Wombats",
      "children": [
        {
